**Definition 14.1 (the added activation relation).** The extension adds exactly
an owner-authorized `Install` entrance at an original post-acquisition
source-ready cut, and the later canonical three service tags. At every
original busy PC Install remains disabled. Activation enters a new invocation of PR57's finite bitwise installation
mechanism by one CTL; it is a declared new admission edge, not an alleged
original source request. The permission includes the two prescribed fresh
control versions $L,D_1$, not an arbitrary dynamic loader. Each native
control version is constructed once. The bootstrap writes one finite
new main-control description bit at a time, then enables that main control.
It does not install a tree or read the private source.

The new instrument's complete primitive table has one entry: at the actual
original Ready, its controller owner, possessing the actual seed/retention
read authorities, may supply the control action Install; execute one CTL
setting the existing finite bootstrap mode/PC to NEXT and making the
source dispatcher unavailable until initialization returns. Its ordinary
cell effect is empty; its meter effect is the one ordinary receipt.
All other PCs have no Install edge. This is a bounded primitive **permission
extension**, with refinement map Install $\mapsto$ that PR57 CTL. It does
not assert a new instruction is already in $D_0$'s current row. The external
actor's menu is extended by this single control input, whose actual occurrence
is retained by the activation record. The phase/PC fields and bootstrap
mechanism already exist; the new code $L$ and $D_1$ must still be installed
by the paid bootstrap before their first row. The instrument is a declared
change to primitive enabledness, not a programme purporting to rewrite its
own enabling rule. Without this added permission the old interface has no
such installation path. No construction of a new physical control port from
old source permissions is claimed. Its ideal primitive price is exactly
one CTL, one receipt, one tick and two fee units; no separate unbounded
loader action is hidden inside it. A model requiring a physical realization
of this permission beyond the declared PR57 ideal interface needs that
additional conformance result.

At replay of the old Ready with the recorded $j_A$ and activation not yet
started, Step selects this recorded primitive instrument row before ordinary
$D_0$ row lookup. Its encoded effect is enter-bootstrap. It then follows
the actual boot, $L$, second boot and $D_1$ rows. This is the only pre-main
external control choice besides IN. Without that explicit recorded-row
selection, looking up the old Ready in $D_0$ alone would replay a source
start instead. After activation every non-IN step is its installed prescribed
row. The prefix state before Install is still the actual old state; the
newly admitted alternative continuation is explicitly the interface change.

Let $D_1$ be the following precisely specified finite construction:
expand Definitions 11.1–13.4, inline the acyclic word macros, expand finite
cases in their displayed order, and join the renamed original five source
paths as in Definition 13.3. Give variables first-occurrence indices;
give native states routine order, then textual occurrence order, with
false successor first. Encode rows by PR57.03 and their finite local-effect
tags by their displayed table order. The finite bootstrap and the fixed
Ready activation/handoff adapters have that same literal specification.
This produces one finite description, independent of $u,H$, and frame
length. $H$ is acquired manifest data. There is no Partrec-code existence
step or unspecified evaluator in this construction.

In particular $D_1$ contains ManifestPrefix's two entry moves and every
BeginField/FieldRead test and compiled word helper in Definition 13.2.
Its actual $\Delta(D_1)$ and $E(D_1)$ serialize that full expansion,
including its register count, row numbering and successors. No encoding
or bit length of a different expansion may be supplied in their place.
The capture controller below uses only the native ingress/descriptor
schema and has no manifest importer or dependency on $D_1$'s bit length.

Use two ordinary bootstrap invocations. The first installs a fixed finite
capture controller $L$. Its native graph is: eight authorized verifier-to-$O$
TOKs; position its owned lanes by Home/Tail; append an F sentinel to
port 37; receive one canonical code
frame on port 37 by Definition 13.2's native Base/bit/length schema,
with destination 37 instead of 38 and its one capture-purpose reserve CTL.
It omits the later service-token admission, Import and word work;
construct that frame's measured Base, length and descriptor;
seal it; then one CTL returns to the existing bootstrap. Omit Import and
all bank/handler work from this capture graph. Its descriptors and scratch
are constructed by the explicit native scans/copies, not the word compiler.
Thus $L$ has no call to the service it is about to load. Its finite encoding
is obtained by PR57.D1's same textual expansion. It does not contain $D_1$.

The captured frame has the fixed three-bit code-kind tag 101, actual
$U(d_1)$ and the $d_1$ bits of $\Delta(D_1)$, including the initial finite
control/token data. Its body is this prescribed archival description of
the finite expansion. The capture controller's real unary
length, bitwise input and append rows pay for this entire acquisition.
It runs the Base/descriptor schema even on its empty initial destination;
port 37 is not treated as a free preconstructed code block.

The second bootstrap installs the corresponding $E(D_1)$ as actual main
control; the capture frame is not mistaken for an unframed PR57 row tape.
Let $m=|E(D_1)|$ for this invocation. Choose the following explicit
five-state bootstrap refinement:
NEXT is CTL testing the finite control-store end; its non-end successor
is INPUT, an IN; WRITE is the one control-bit WR boot variant; ADVANCE
is CTL moving to the next finite control-bit slot, returning to NEXT;
the end successor is DONE, one CTL enabling the installed main control.
Thus $m$ bits cost $4m+2$ occurrences. This uses PR57.D1's stated
five-state bitwise bootstrap with the explicit fresh-version permission;
ordinary port boundaries already exist from the original boot. The finite control store in each invocation is the prescribed literal
version's slot sequence, not an unbounded ordinary tape or an acquired
integer in a latch. A boot-WR installs exactly one previously uninstalled
finite control bit, with the same one-bit constructor convention as
PR57.D1's original installation; it adds that bit to $b_{\rm ctl}$. The
fixed NEXT/ADVANCE mechanism selects its successor slot in that finite
version. Old version slots remain retained as inactive control material;
only DONE changes which complete row table is active. Control-bit storage
and the finite slot cursor are included in the actual installed encoding,
not an ordinary cell assumed to have been allocated for free. There are
no new ordinary boundary pairs: all 63 were already constructed in the
original boot. Hence there are no unspecified boundary-constructor rows
in either later bootstrap. Re-enabling this fresh-version bitwise mechanism
is part of the declared installation permission; PR57's original once-only
boot does not by itself grant that re-entry. A model whose boot-WR cannot
construct these fresh finite version bits does not satisfy this extension.
There is no unbounded numeral in a latch. In particular the capture graph's extra
rows do not execute before the first bootstrap has actually installed them.

Valid installation fixes all three public words to independently computed
$E(L)$, the captured $\Delta(D_1)$ frame and $E(D_1)$ at the second control
bootstrap. These are paid canonical code-supply inputs, not private-source
advice. The installed native code must equal the specified serialization
of the captured description, as a displayed canonical-installation condition,
just as the original boot fixes its literal installed controller. The construction does not claim
that an arbitrary replacement program can certify its own code. Supplying
a different word leaves the valid-version domain; it is not a new valid
version silently replayed with the old description. Every such code IN is
either the prescribed $E(L)$ bit, a retained $\Delta(D_1)$ frame bit, or
the corresponding prescribed $E(D_1)$ bit, all determined by this finite compilation. No other boot choice
is omitted from the manifest.

Before activation $D_0$ and its bootstrap determine the complete old
prefix. The additional permission to enter bootstrap changes the admission
relation. It is not claimed to be a transition already in $M_0$. Through
the cut just before that activation occurrence, actual cells, latches,
PC, source, meter and native code are exactly the old $M_0$ prefix.
The new control bits, assets and all later receipts first belong to the
installation suffix. The irreducible primitive bootstrap convention is
explicitly the same model foundation as PR57.D1; no free loader, pause,
unbounded native write or extra port is added.

